      <tr><td>3/8 inch</td><td>6–8 hours</td><td>Thick, meaty</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Ground beef (gun)</td><td>5–7 hours</td><td>Uniform sticks</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>How Long to Dehydrate Beef Jerky at 160°F</h2>
  <p>At 160°F — the USDA-recommended temperature — beef jerky sliced at 1/4 inch takes <strong>4–6 hours</strong> to fully dehydrate. The range exists because dehydrators vary in power and airflow efficiency, and humidity in your kitchen affects drying speed. Check at 4 hours and every 30–45 minutes after.</p>

  <h2>How Long to Dehydrate Beef Jerky at 145°F</h2>
  <p>At 145°F, drying takes 6–9 hours — but this is <strong>not recommended for food safety</strong>. The USDA only considers beef jerky safe when the internal temperature reaches 160°F. If your dehydrator only reaches 145°F, finish strips in a 275°F oven for 10 minutes to ensure food safety.</p>

